var reportResearchDigest = []string{
	"Brand Recognition vs. Discovery. A key framework throughout LLM Optimizer is the distinction between brand recognition -- how well AI represents your brand when people search for it by name -- and inbound discovery -- how often AI surfaces your brand when people search your category without prior knowledge of you. Both matter, but they require different strategies. Brand recognition improves through authority signals, earned media, and training data presence. Discovery requires appearing in category-level content, answering the questions your audience asks before they know you exist, and being present in the YouTube videos, Wikipedia pages, review sites, comparison lists and community threads that AI assistants draw on for category queries.",
	"The emerging science of LLM visibility distinguishes two ways a brand becomes known to AI. Parametric visibility is what a model learned in training; dynamic visibility is what it finds when it searches the web while answering. NanoKnow (SIGIR 2026) shows, on small open models, that how often an answer appears in the training data strongly affects whether a model knows it, and that retrieved evidence and training knowledge complement each other. Both matter in practice: ChatGPT searched the web for about 34.5% of US prompts in early 2026 and answered the rest from what it had learned. The dual imperative holds: be published widely enough to be learned, and be retrievable and citable when assistants search.",
	"What a model remembers shapes even its searched answers. In a University of Toronto study of 158,000 answers collected in mid-2026, ChatGPT wrote a brand into its own search queries 67% of the time when the brand was in its prior knowledge, against 11% otherwise, and 46% of its unprompted brand mentions had no supporting cited page. Familiarity compounds: the most prominent brands were cited 1.4-2.8 times as often as the average brand, web retrieval didn't correct this, and fact-preserving rewrites gave negligible gains. In one vendor panel, household names appeared in 73% of relevant category answers, mid-market brands in 44% and niche brands in 11%, so compare a brand with peers of similar size, not with category leaders. Category leaders reach nearly every retrieval but win only 25-41% of the recommendation slots they reach, so they need differentiation; about half of specialist and regional brands never surfaced at all, so they first need to be found. When two providers both left a brand out, they agreed 95% of the time on why: it never reached the model, reached it but wasn't mentioned, or was mentioned but not positioned as the pick. For challengers, third-party signals that build familiarity matter more than rewrites: with identical specifications, well-known brands won every recommendation until a competitor had even a small rating advantage (under 0.1 stars).",
	"Across the research, AI answers cite third parties far more than a brand's own pages: independent sources made up 72-92% of citations in the Toronto study, and 85.7% of citations in brand answers across 13 languages pointed to sites the brand doesn't own. Much of that third-party share is other companies' commercial pages and ranked \"best X\" listicles, not only editorial media: in one vendor panel the tracked brand's own domain drew 2.9% of citations. Owned pages still matter. In another vendor panel, a brand was named in about 3% of searched answers when neither it nor its site came up, about half the time when one of its own pages was cited, and almost always when the engine also searched its name. Self-ranked lists are a weak lever: self-promotional listicles drew about 11% of citations in software reviews (3.6% in ChatGPT), but when a brand's own \"best X\" list was cited in AI Overviews, the brand was left out of the recommendation 69% of the time; third-party lists are what count. Video is part of third-party coverage: YouTube is the most-cited domain in Google AI Overviews (24.5%) and leads in aggregate across assistants, mainly through Google's AI surfaces, while Wikipedia leads in Claude and Copilot and is overrepresented in AI summaries; per-engine rankings depend on the prompt set. Because video LLMs learn from transcripts, not pictures, what is said in a video matters far more than its production value.",
	"What has not held up is the 2023 headline that adding quotations raises visibility by 41%. It was a lab result: C-SEO Bench, a 2026 survey of 45 studies and a re-test on ten current engine families (by vendor-affiliated authors, with code and data released) found the quotation, statistics and cite-sources levers moved citation on none of them. Page features that look helpful when comparing websites -- FAQ blocks, summary boxes, schema markup -- showed no effect when comparing pages of the same website. What held up was fit to the question: pages whose title and key paragraph match the question people ask were cited about 14-16% more per standard deviation, and which pages an engine's own search retrieved explained far more of the variation than any page feature. Being cited is also not the same as shaping the answer: pages containing figures, definitions, comparisons and how-to steps had markedly more influence on the answer than pages without them, while Q&A formatting had slightly less, and explicit prices and recent dates helped in a controlled test. The levers that reproduce are relevance, prominence and specific, usable evidence, so a page score measures quality and fit for the question, not a predicted citation rate.",
	"Reddit's role is shifting, and how much it counts depends on how it is measured. It was foundational training data through WebText and the Common Crawl, and Google and OpenAI signed data licensing deals with it in 2024, which are reported to be under renegotiation. Its upvoted consensus, especially in \"best X for Y\" threads, still shapes how models describe a category. But its share of AI citations fell about 50% between October 2025 and January 2026, and per-engine figures disagree widely: Ahrefs' November 2026 prompt set put Reddit second in ChatGPT at 6.2%, while Meltwater's October 2026 data had ChatGPT near 0.4% Reddit and Claude at none, with Reddit Gemini's top domain until Gemini's Reddit citations fell 88% in late October. Citation share understates its influence: in Ahrefs' study of 1.4 million ChatGPT prompts, Reddit made up 67.8% of the pages ChatGPT retrieved but did not cite. Genuine participation is worth it; over-investing in Reddit at the expense of other channels is not.",
	"Google AI Overviews and standalone assistants draw on different sources, and AI Overviews lean less on rankings than they did. About 38% of AI Overview citations come from top-10 organic pages (Ahrefs, March 2026; 76% in its 2025 study, partly because of a change in how results are parsed), on average 53% of the domains AI Overviews consult are outside the organic top 10, and Google's results, AI Overviews and Gemini share few sources. Gemini 3 has powered AI Overviews since January 2026, with a handoff into AI Mode, which passed 1 billion monthly users. Clicks fall where AI answers appear: the top organic result loses about 58% of its clicks under an AI Overview, a field experiment found AI Overviews cut organic clicks by 38%, and in a preregistered experiment an AI Mode-only search cut click-through by 18.8 percentage points (21.2 points for clicks to Reddit). Visibility increasingly shows up as being named, not as visits. For standalone assistants, only 12% of cited URLs rank in Google's top 10. Across 75,000 brands, YouTube mentions (about 0.74) and branded web mentions (0.66-0.71) correlate most strongly with AI visibility and Domain Rating far less (0.27-0.33) -- correlation, not causation. Freshness matters too: AI assistants cite content 25.7% newer than search results do, and how recently key commercial and comparison pages were updated matters more than a site's average content age.",
	"Crawlers now differ by purpose. OpenAI's GPTBot collects training data only; OAI-SearchBot fetches pages for ChatGPT search, and ChatGPT-User, which fetches pages a user asks about, may not follow robots.txt. Blocking training crawlers is a business choice, but blocking search crawlers hides a site from AI answers. Google-Extended controls whether Google may use a site for Gemini training and grounding; Google says it does not affect inclusion or ranking in Google Search, and appearing in AI Overviews needs only an indexed page eligible for a snippet. A SIGIR 2026 study found that sites blocking Google's AI crawler appear less in AI Overview retrieval, but that is an association: the sites that block AI crawlers are disproportionately large, reputable publishers. Google intends to support the AIPREF training opt-out signal in early 2027. robots.txt states intent but doesn't enforce it: in a controlled 2026 study Claude and Mistral followed it, while Gemini and Copilot fetched disallowed pages. Training is now 52% of crawler requests and mixed-use crawlers over 36%, which makes clean training-only blocks hard, and since 15 September 2026 Cloudflare blocks AI crawlers by default on new domains, so a site can be hidden from AI without its owner choosing it. Two popular tactics showed no effect on live engines in 2026: schema markup gave no meaningful citation lift (it still helps rich results and entity understanding, and helped retrieval in one simulated pipeline), and 97% of llms.txt files got no requests from AI crawlers; Google says no special files or schema are needed.",
	"AI answers are not reliably grounded, and how often depends on the topic. 11% of AI Overview claims in general queries were not supported by the sources they cited, against about 43% of cited statements in health answers, and 45% of AI assistant answers about news had at least one significant issue. Errors about brands cluster where they hurt: in one vendor dataset of 158,000 checked claims, pricing and billing were 12% of claims but 24% of the inaccurate ones, and 54% of brands had at least one error that cited their own pages, so explicit, current pricing pages and an audit of old owned pages are worth the effort. Citation still concentrates: the top 20 sources capture 28-67% of citations, and LLMs attend most to the beginning and end of their context, so front-loading answers still pays.",
	"Answers also vary from run to run. Even at temperature 0, 9-27% of yes/no answers flipped within five minutes, and only 18% of the pages AI Overviews used were the same two months later, against 45% for organic results; asked the same question repeatedly, AI tools returned the same list of brands less than 1% of the time. Brand-level rates averaged over many questions are reliable after a few runs, but a difference on a single question is only indicative at five runs -- detecting it reliably takes 15-25 runs. Sentiment is the least stable measure: it flipped about 6.7 times as often as mention in one vendor panel, and automated sentiment scores varied far more with the query's language (26% of variance) than with the brand (1.5%). The interface matters too: for the same question, a chatbot's API and its consumer app shared only 12-15% of cited domains, and providers recommend from memory at very different rates. Visibility is therefore best measured as a distribution over repeated runs, per engine -- how often a brand is mentioned, not where it ranks in a list -- and the figures here describe associations, not causes.",
	"Model updates add volatility. When GPT-5.3 replaced GPT-4o as ChatGPT's default, unique domains cited per response dropped 20.5% overnight, and in October 2026 alone Gemini cut its links per answer and Perplexity trimmed its listed sources, so per-engine shares are cited here with dates, not built into scores. This reinforces the value of parametric visibility alongside dynamic visibility; research into LLM parametric memory suggests that being associated with category leaders can matter more than raw mention frequency. Together, these findings inform LLM Optimizer's scoring frameworks across answer optimization, video authority, Reddit authority and search visibility. The LLM Visibility Score weighs the LLM Test -- the direct measure of what assistants answer -- as heavily as answer optimization (25% each; video 20%, Reddit 15%, search 15%). The LLM Test asks each question several times through the providers' APIs, reports sentiment separately from its score, and with web search on shows why a brand is missed: never found, found but not named, or named but not recommended.",
}

